$DIR/main.p:28:2: cannot pass &u to fmt.Println, which keeps it after returning
    &u points into u. u is a local variable of f (declared at main.p:26).
    fmt.Println passes a pointer reachable from args to Fprintln
    (print_linux.p:17), which passes a pointer reachable from args to
    (*printer).doPrint (fmt.p:948), which passes a pointer reachable from args
    to (*printer).printArg (fmt.p:915), which passes a pointer reachable from
    arg to a formatting method of what it walks (which may call
    main.(*T).String) (fmt.p:671), which stores a pointer reachable from t in
    package variable kept (main.p:18), so &u would outlive u.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    If fmt.Println should keep its own copy of u, change it to take the value,
    not a pointer, or an owner it can keep: an owned[U] from new, a shared[U],
    or a library owner such as a vec.Vec.
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3003" for a longer explanation)
